  "body": "**Stacked on #975 (on #964).** This branch starts at #975's head (`issue/966-lathe-c-no-rate`, which sits on #964's `issue/906-c-unwind-rate-cap`), because all three edit `assets/posts/fanuc-lathe.cps`. Until they merge, this diff also shows their commits. This PR's own change is the single commit `fix(post): the Fanuc lathe leaves a face-drilled hole at the stream's exit`. Retarget/merge order follows AGENTS.md \"Overlapping issues stack\": #964, then #975, then this one, after `git merge origin/main`.\n\n## What changed\n\n`assets/posts/fanuc-lathe.cps` swallowed every move after a face cycle (G83/G84/G85) until the section ended. So the chain's exit rapid up to clearance never posted: the tool left the hole at R, and `G28 U0 W0` (X and Z together) started from below the verified path.\n\nThe fix applies #994's hop law (PR #1019, for the mill posts) to the lathe:\n- The cycle replaces only the chain that stays on its hole: the hole's XY (on centre), at or below R (`onHole`).\n- The first rapid that leaves the hole ends the group with `G80` and is posted as the stream says (`chainRapid`).\n- A feed or arc that leaves the hole inside an open group is refused by name: \"a cutting move leaves its hole inside a face-cycle group\" (`chainCut`).\n- A legacy cycle record with no R word keeps the whole on-axis chain swallowed, as before.\n\n`assets/posts/tormach-lathe.cps` doesn't have the bug and is unchanged. It writes no canned cycle and posts the whole chain as plain moves, so its `axial_drill_mm.nc` / `axial_drill_inch.nc` goldens already end with `G0 X0 Z30` before `G30`. The new read-back pin runs on it too.\n\nThe decisions (and what was dropped) are in `docs/decisions/1011-lathe-cycle-exit.md`.\n\n## Before / after (`crates/app/tests/golden/nc/fanuc-lathe/axial_drill.nc`, clearance 30, R 20)\n\n```\n G00 X0 Z20\n G83 Z10 Q12000 F0.008\n G80\n+G00 X0 Z30\n M9\n M05\n G28 U0 W0\n```\n\n## Goldens\n\nOnly one golden moved, and I read it: `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/fanuc-lathe/axial_drill.nc`, which gains the exit `G00 X0 Z30`. `WRITE_GOLDEN=1` over `lathe_post`, `lathe_post_tormach` and `lathe_post_refusals` changed nothing else. No other committed program or test text contains a lathe face-cycle word.\n\n## Tests\n\n- New `crates/app/tests/lathe_cycle_exit.rs`:\n  - `both_lathe_posts_leave_a_face_drilled_hole_at_the_streams_exit` posts the axial-drill document's stream through both `fanuc-lathe.cps` and `tormach-lathe.cps`. A read-back checker follows each program as a control would: a face cycle returns to the level it was read at, G80 moves nothing, G28/G30 go home. It asserts one hole drilled, no X move below the stream's exit (its `ClearancePlane`, 30) after the hole, and the tool at the exit level or higher when it leaves the cut (at the first home move).\n  - `the_checker_catches_the_r_plane_exit` pins the checker itself on the #1011 program, an off-centre move at R, and the two good shapes (cycle and plain moves).\n- New `lathe_post_refusals::a_face_cycle_ends_on_the_rapid_leaving_its_hole_and_refuses_a_cut_that_leaves` uses a synthetic stream. The exit rapid posts as `G83 ...` / `G80` / `G00 X0 Z40`, with exactly one group `G80`. A feed off the hole inside the group is refused by name and writes no guess.\n- **Revert check:** with the old `fanuc-lathe.cps` restored, `both_lathe_posts_leave_a_face_drilled_hole_at_the_streams_exit` fails (`\"the program leaves its hole at Z20, below the exit Z30\"`, fanuc-lathe) and the refusals test fails on the missing `G00 X0 Z40`. With the fix, both pass.\n- Quick gate: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` on the final head. The golden change maps it to every app suite, which includes lathe_post, lathe_post_refusals, lathe_post_g76, lathe_post_tormach, lathe_post_tormach_thread, lathe_spindle_law, c_axis_live, c_axis_unwind, posting, golden_cl, conformance, drilling_axial, drill_prior and drill_prior_kinds. Result: green, 1 step, 0 failed, 25.8 min (`gate-logs/quick-20261112-023802`). Clippy on camcraft-app tests: `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --test lathe_cycle_exit --test lathe_post_refusals -- -D warnings` clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- A lathe tap (G84) or bore (G85) document end to end. They share the same `onRapid`/`chainRapid` path as G83, which the synthetic test covers.\n- Mill-turn C-axis sections. A cycle there is still refused before any group opens, and that is unchanged.\n- The positioning block (`G00 X0 Z<R>`, written only when the stream's approach isn't already there) is not re-seated the way #994's `cycleSeat` does it. A lathe drills one on-axis hole, and the axial-drill stream always arrives on R first. Noted in the decision file.\n- `assets/posts/AGENTS.md`'s hop-law paragraph is #1019's, so I didn't edit it here. The lathe post states the rule in its own header.\n\nChangelog: The Fanuc lathe post leaves a face-drilled hole at the stream's exit level (G80, then the exit rapid) instead of homing from the R plane; a cut leaving the hole inside a face cycle is refused by name (#1011)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFork synced at v0.17.0 (PROCESS_VERSION 16).\n\nFixes #1011\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
